### Coreconf-m2m
- **Presenter:** Laurent Toutain
- **Slides:** [Coreconf-m2m](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-t2trg-02/materials/slides-interim-2026-t2trg-02-sessa-coreconf-m2m-00)

#### Overview & Discussion
- Laurent Toutain presented work from the Terraforma project in France, addressing the challenge of mapping heterogeneous sensor data into domain ontologies (such as Open Geospatial Consortium / SOSA) without relying on brittle, centralized translation pipelines.
- The proposed architecture deploys IP to constrained devices using SCHC compression over LPWANs (e.g., LoRaWAN) and defines a stateless coreconf-m2m data model based on CORECONF and SID allocations.
- Coreconf-m2m structures payload representations into bootstrap (start time, inventory, type references), characteristics, transducers (sensors, actuators, values, statistics), and notification objects (history series, threshold alerts).
- Binary SID representations are mapped to human- and LLM-readable textual names via DNS queries. Laurent demonstrated mapping the data store into Semantic Definition Format (SDF) and querying via Model Context Protocol (MCP) agents and LLMs.
- Implementation measurements on Zephyr OS showed a footprint of approximately 120 KB, where the compressed network stack (IPv6/UDP/CoAP/CORECONF via SCHC) occupies only 10% of the code space.

#### Q&A & Chat Discussion
- Lorenzo Corneo noted in chat that the mapping aligns well with SDF protocol mappings, which can be extended via registries.
- Carsten Bormann noted RFC 9006 in chat as relevant prior work for TCP usage in constrained IoT environments.
- Manu Gupta asked about MCP server agentic interoperability. Laurent Toutain clarified that the MCP setup demonstrates converting numerical representations into semantic names that LLMs can process, rather than dynamic resource discovery.

