### Resilience as a Protocol: Measurement, Analytics, Trust Signals, and Verifiable Network Intelligence for AI Agents, IoT Systems, and Decentralized Networks
- **Presenters:** Johanna Rooney, Manu Gupta
- **Slides:** [Resilience as a Protocol: Measurement, Analytics, Trust Signals, and Verifiable Network Intelligence for AI Agents, IoT Systems, and Decentralized Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-t2trg-02/materials/slides-interim-2026-t2trg-02-sessa-resilience-as-a-protocol-measurement-analytics-trust-signals-and-verifiable-network-intelligence-for-ai-agents-iot-systems-and-d-00)

#### Overview & Discussion
- Johanna Rooney and Manu Gupta introduced a framework to treat network resilience as a first-class protocol signal rather than a post-hoc operational concern.
- While discovery protocols (CoAP, Core Link Format, SDF) expose device semantics, AI agents remain unaware of underlying network failures (e.g., routing changes, network partitions).
- The proposed architecture captures real-time telemetry from decentralized substrates (Kademlia DHT, libp2p) measuring reachability and churn. Operational telemetry is encoded as CBOR objects, mapped into SDF blocks, and hashed into SHA-256 Content Identifiers (CIDs) via IPLD for cross-organizational cryptographic verification.
- Real-time resilience signals enable AI agents operating on top of SOSA/SAREF ontologies to dynamically re-evaluate environment health, halt unsafe commands, or migrate compute placements between edge and cloud.

#### Q&A
- Carsten Bormann raised the question of evaluating redundant infrastructure (backup path availability) alongside active reachability. Manu Gupta explained that their system uses multi-addrs, transport security (AutoTLS, NOISE), and pub-sub mechanisms (GossipSub, FloodSub) to monitor peer availability, noting that routing layer abstractions must be designed to expose resiliency metrics.
- Ari Keränen asked about performance in non-DHT environments. Manu Gupta noted that in networks where peer-to-peer traffic is restricted, the framework falls back to HTTP-based MCP servers and Agent-to-Agent (A2A) interactions.

