### Application-Centric Considerations for Deployment over the IoT Computing Continuum
- **Presenter:** Carlos Kamienski
- **Slides:** [Application-Centric Considerations for Deployment over the IoT Computing Continuum](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-t2trg-02/materials/slides-interim-2026-t2trg-02-sessa-application-centric-considerations-for-deployment-over-the-iot-computing-continuum-00)

#### Overview & Discussion
- Carlos Kamienski discussed lessons learned from multi-year smart agriculture deployments and drone delivery simulations regarding service placement over the computing continuum.
- Building on RFC 9556 (IoT Edge Computing Challenges and Functions), applications are modeled as Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) of microservices. Placement maps map functions across multi-tier stages (constrained devices/mist, edge/farm office, cloud) subject to SLAs, intermittent connectivity, and node availability.
- Deployment management can operate via centralized orchestration or distributed choreography using AI agents to negotiate function locations.
- The presentation raised whether guidelines or standard specifications should be defined for managing service placement graphs across heterogeneous continuum stages.

#### Q&A
- Manu Gupta inquired about transport protocols and cross-organizational identity interoperability, suggesting simulation scenarios where identity signals mitigate unauthorized drone operations. Carlos Kamienski clarified that their simulation models currently focus on dense drone collision avoidance and abstract lower-layer transport and identity details.
- Johanna Rooney asked about physical testing with drones. Carlos explained that physical drones are used for agricultural data collection, whereas collision avoidance for delivery is kept to simulations due to cost and safety constraints.
- Ari Keränen noted interest in seeing the underlying implementation stack and comparing it against composable code architectures.

